# Cloudflare Workers static-assets header overrides (Session 29).
#
# service-worker.js's whole job is detecting when it's outdated — an
# aggressively cached copy of the file itself defeats that before the
# browser's own update-check logic (Session 27's skipWaiting()/
# clients.claim() fix) ever gets a chance to run. Real symptom this
# responds to: Session 27's code-level fix was verified correct, but
# Elad still needed a hard refresh after the next deploy.
#
# Confirmed, not assumed, before picking a fix: curled the real live
# URL (https://thescanner.lanirelad.workers.dev/service-worker.js) and
# found `Cache-Control: public, max-age=0, must-revalidate` (Cloudflare
# Workers' own documented default for static assets) *and*
# `CF-Cache-Status: HIT` on the same response — Cloudflare's edge is
# serving this file straight from cache without even reaching the
# origin, despite max-age=0. `must-revalidate`/`max-age=0` is a browser-
# facing instruction; it does not by itself stop Cloudflare's own edge
# from caching the response at all.
#
# `no-store`, not `no-cache`: tried `no-cache` first, but Cloudflare's
# own documented behavior treats `no-cache` as "cache it, but check
# with the origin before serving" — which still means an edge cache
# entry exists, and evidently didn't stop the observed HIT. `no-store`
# is Cloudflare's own documented way to make the edge (and the browser)
# skip caching this response entirely — the only directive strong
# enough to guarantee "the edge always goes to origin for this file."
#
# Scoped to this one file only — every other static asset (styles.css,
# app.js, icons, real_mascot.png, the JSON data exports) keeps
# Cloudflare's normal caching behavior; none of them need this, and
# over-applying it would undercut the whole point of a cache-first PWA
# shell (ARCHITECTURE.md §9a).
/service-worker.js
  Cache-Control: no-store
